From: Herman van Hazendonk <github.com@herrie.org>
Date: Wed, 18 Nov 2026 14:15:40 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] ALSA: pcm: treat a stop threshold of UINT_MAX as never stop

MediaTek's audio HAL opens the modem's PCM streams through tinyalsa with
stop_threshold set to 0xffffffff, meaning the stream must never stop on
an underrun. tinyalsa holds that value in an unsigned int.

Android runs this HAL as a 32-bit process. pcm_compat keeps a 32-bit
caller's boundary below 2^31, so the value is at or above the boundary
and snd_pcm_playback_data() lets an empty stream start. A 64-bit build of
the same HAL, as loaded into PulseAudio through libhybris, gets the 64-bit
boundary. The value is then far below it, and SNDRV_PCM_IOCTL_START on the
empty playback stream fails:

  AudioPcmControl: pcm_start fail due to cannot start channel: Broken pipe

The call then has no audio. Take UINT_MAX to mean the boundary, which is
how the same request already behaves for a 32-bit caller.

Signed-off-by: Herman van Hazendonk <github.com@herrie.org>
Upstream-Status: Pending
---
 sound/core/pcm_native.c | 10 ++++++++++
 1 file changed, 10 insertions(+)

diff --git a/sound/core/pcm_native.c b/sound/core/pcm_native.c
index 78e3874..ad183e2 100644
--- a/sound/core/pcm_native.c
+++ b/sound/core/pcm_native.c
@@ -916,6 +916,15 @@ static int snd_pcm_sw_params(struct snd_pcm_substream *substream,
 		if (params->silence_threshold > runtime->buffer_size)
 			return -EINVAL;
 	}
+	/*
+	 * tinyalsa holds the thresholds in an unsigned int, and callers ask
+	 * it for a stream that never stops with UINT_MAX. That reaches the
+	 * boundary of a 32-bit caller, which pcm_compat keeps below 2^31, but
+	 * not the boundary of a 64-bit one.
+	 */
+	if (params->stop_threshold == UINT_MAX)
+		params->stop_threshold = runtime->boundary;
+
 	err = 0;
 	snd_pcm_stream_lock_irq(substream);
 	runtime->tstamp_mode = params->tstamp_mode;
